  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 241 PM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nA ridge of high pressure at the surface nosing into eastern Kansas \nwill bring a stable post-frontal regime to the area tonight and \nSaturday. As this ridge translates eastward return flow will \ngradually develop allowing southerly winds to return to the region \nfrom west to east as we move through the day on Saturday. Another \nshortwave trough racing eastward along the Canadian border will \ndrive a cold front south late Sat shifting winds to the \nnorth/northeast on Sunday. This will allow another ridge of high \npressure to build in Sunday night, allowing for strong radiational \ncooling. This will result in below average minimum temperatures on \nSunday morning with low-lying areas falling into the mid 40s in some \nlocations.\n\nTranquil weather will persist as we head into next week. Northwest \nmid/upper flow will prevail early in the week leading to dry and \nbreezy conditions with above average temperatures anticipated. \nAfternoon highs are expected to rise into the low to mid 80s by mid \nweek and persist through the end of the week. Another progressive \nshortwave trough racing eastward along the Canadian border will \ndrive a subtle frontal boundary southward across the northern Plains \nlate Tue with little or no impact to sensible weather across the \nCentral Plains. This feature may be followed by another late \nThursday into Friday but we continue to see some uncertainty in how \nfar south the cold front plunges leading to a continuation of mild \nand tranquil weather across the central Plains.",
